ORCHIDEE-FORGE simulation, an agricultural carrying capacity, a c.200 AD census or a ranking of societal development.","sources":[{"id":"zhu2021","authors":"Zhu, Galbraith, Reyes-Garcia and Ciais","year":2021,"title":"Global hunter-gatherer population densities constrained by influence of seasonality on diet composition","doi":"10.1038/s41559-021-01548-3","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41559-021-01548-3","full_text":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7611941/","input_data":"https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.14995320.v2","use":"Human foraging, food intake, energy reserve and demographic equations; edible-plant allocation. Original animal/vegetation forcing is not reproduced by this adapter.","used_for":"Human foraging, food intake, energy reserve and demographic equations; edible-plant allocation. Original animal/vegetation forcing is not reproduced by this adapter."},{"id":"tallavaara2018","authors":"Tallavaara, Eronen and Luoto","year":2018,"title":"Productivity, biodiversity, and pathogens influence the global hunter-gatherer population density","doi":"10.1073/pnas.1715638115","url":"https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1715638115","data_and_script":"https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.1167852","repository_license":"CC BY 4.0","use":"Research comparison only. Its empirical biodiversity and ten-pathogen predictors are unavailable for the fantasy geography; Miami NPP plus malaria is not an implementation of its fitted model.","used_for":"Research comparison only. Its empirical biodiversity and ten-pathogen predictors are unavailable for the fantasy geography; Miami NPP plus malaria is not an implementation of its fitted model."},{"id":"ordonez2022","authors":"Ordonez and Riede","year":2022,"title":"Changes in limiting factors for forager population dynamics in Europe across the last glacial-interglacial transition","doi":"10.1038/s41467-022-32750-x","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-022-32750-x","use":"Research context: ecological constraints and their relative importance can vary through space and time. No coefficients from this paper are used.","used_for":"Research context: ecological constraints and their relative importance can vary through space and time. No coefficients from this paper are used."},{"id":"matranga2024","authors":"Matranga","year":2024,"title":"The Ant and the Grasshopper: Seasonality and the Invention of Agriculture","doi":"10.1093/qje/qjae012","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/qje/qjae012","use":"Research context for future explicit storage experiments; plant biomass turnover in the current adapter is not a human storage model. No adoption equation from this paper is implemented.","used_for":"Research context for future explicit storage experiments; plant biomass turnover in the current adapter is not a human storage model. No adoption equation from this paper is implemented."},{"title":"Binford (2001) hunter-gatherer data, CRAN package binford 0.1.0 (Marwick, Johnson, White, Eff; GPL-3)","url":"https://cran.r-project.org/package=binford","used_for":"Earth test and fitting of the aquatic and terrestrial resource scales"},{"title":"Natural Earth 1:10m rivers, lake centrelines and lakes","url":"https://www.naturalearthdata.com/","used_for":"freshwater index at the Earth test sites"}],"published_human_parameters":{"body_mass_kg":50.0,"search_area_m2_per_hour":4000.0,"maximum_capture_efficiency":1.0,"crowding_coefficient_standard":200.0,"crowding_coefficient_text_alternative":100.0,"basal_energy_mj_per_day":8.37,"gathering_energy_mj_per_hour":1.25,"hunting_energy_mj_per_hour":1.25,"plant_food_energy_mj_per_kg_dry_matter":9.8,"animal_food_energy_mj_per_kg_dry_matter":9.8,"fat_catabolism_mj_per_kg":39.3,"fat_anabolism_mj_per_kg":54.6,"maximum_fat_reserve_kg":15.0,"minimum_foraging_hours_per_day":0.5,"maximum_foraging_hours_per_day":8.0,"foraging_hours_adjustment_per_day":1.0,"meat_preference_coefficient":2.5,"minimum_hunting_time_fraction":0.05,"maximum_hunting_time_fraction":0.95,"low_meat_intake_trigger_fraction":0.1,"edible_plant_fraction":0.015,"npp_fruit_allocation_fraction":0.065,"minimum_grass_belowground_allocation_fraction":0.2,"birth_rate_maximum_per_year":0.1,"birth_sigmoid_steepness":15.0,"birth_sigmoid_midpoint_reserve_fraction":0.5,"starvation_reserve_standard_deviation_fraction":0.125,"background_mortality_per_year":0.0125,"annual_reproduction_energy_mj_per_birth_rate":478.0,"population_numerical_floor_persons_per_km2":0.001},"published_parameter_notes":["The intake subsection gives crowding c=100, whereas the paper's final standard-configuration paragraph gives c=200. Use and identify the chosen configuration rather than treating the text as unambiguous.","Published plant production is 0.015 * (0.065 * total NPP + belowground fraction * grass NPP). The belowground fraction is max(0.2, (88.3 - 0.0534 * annual precipitation mm) / 100). NPP must be in dry-matter units consistent with the food pools.","Original accessible prey stock uses an independently simulated grazer/browser density, a 180 kg live animal, accessible fraction 0.1 and live-to-edible-dry-mass fraction 0.28. The atlas adapter has no such herbivore simulation.","Original population floor is a numerical reinoculation, not evidence of actual habitation. Empty or failing habitats must not be advertised as populated by that floor.","Food density is kg dry matter per m2. Human density is persons per km2 and must be divided by one million when constructing human biomass and removing food."],"adapter":{"year_days":365,"grass_npp_fraction":0.5,"plant_decay_day":0.02,"annual_meat_fraction_of_npp":0.0005,"phenology_temperature_floor_c":0.0,"phenology_temperature_span_c":15.0,"phenology_rain_half_saturation_mm":50.0,"animal_supply":"annual_stock_pulse","reset_meat_each_year":true},"adapter_assumptions":{"npp":"The existing Miami estimate is annual g dry matter per m2 per year. Multiplication by 1000 converts it to kg dry matter per km2 for the kernel; equivalently divide by 1000 for kg per m2, then multiply by one million m2 per km2. Normalized seasonal weights preserve annual input, but do not reconstruct daily photosynthesis, ORCHIDEE allocation or weather variability.","grass_npp_fraction":"A globally fixed 0.5 is an uncalibrated mixed-vegetation scenario. It is not a biome-specific grass-cover observation. No arbitrary biome-to-density ranking is introduced.","plant_decay_day":"A fixed daily loss fraction of 0.02 is an uncalibrated food-resource turnover scenario. It is not a published universal FORGE parameter and is not harvested-food storage or technological preservation.","annual_meat_fraction_of_npp":"A globally fixed 0.0005 of annual dry-matter NPP supplies an illustrative accessible prey budget. It replaces missing grazer/browser and trophic dynamics, has not been calibrated against herbivore or human observations, and does not reproduce the published accessible-animal fraction by itself.","annual_prey_stock":"The proxy prey budget is supplied once at the start of the year; hunting depletes it. Residual prey is discarded and separately accounted before annual replacement. There is no hidden daily recruitment. This schedule is inspired by FORGE's annual stock update, but omits animal births, mortality and feedback from human hunting.","phenology":"Temperature floor/span and rainfall half-saturation are transparent uncalibrated seasonal resource proxies. When all seasonal weights vanish, use a documented uniform fallback while retaining the annual NPP budget; do not manufacture an observed growing season.","land_use":"Treat this as a separate wild-food counterfactual. Do not add its full-land potential to full-land crop or pasture support, which would allocate the same land and primary production more than once."},"limitations":["No reproduced ORCHIDEE vegetation, plant functional types, soil carbon, grazer/browser demography or original global FORGE calibration.","Monthly mean climate cannot resolve daily weather or resource covariance. Any daily timing is a scenario interpolation.","No small game, migration, trade, food sharing between cells, institutions or crop production is represented by these foraging maps. Fish and other aquatic foods enter as one coastal and freshwater supply (aquatic block), fitted on North American foragers and tested on the rest of the world; seasonal fish runs and food storage are not represented.","Environmental pathogen exposure is not inferred from meat or foraging effort. The Tallavaara empirical pathogen predictor is not the atlas malaria index.","Foraging density and effort concern subsistence under stated resources. They do not predict surplus appropriation, social complexity, innovation or political development.","Alternative resource parameters are sensitivity scenarios, not calibrated probabilities or statistical confidence intervals."],"kernel_parameters":{"basal_mj_day":8.37,"activity_mj_hour":1.25,"food_mj_kg_dm":9.8,"body_mass_kg":50.0,"max_fat_kg":15.0,"fat_deficit_mj_kg":39.3,"fat_surplus_mj_kg":54.6,"search_area_m2_hour":4000.0,"maximum_efficiency":1.0,"crowding":200.0,"min_hours":0.5,"max_hours":8.0,"hours_step":1.0,"initial_hours":4.0,"carnivory_response":2.5,"min_hunt_fraction":0.05,"max_hunt_fraction":0.95,"meat_fraction_floor":0.1,"maximum_birth_rate":0.1,"birth_sigmoid":15.0,"starvation_sd_fat_fraction":0.125,"background_mortality_year":0.0125,"reproduction_mj_birth":478.0,"population_floor_people_km2":0.001,"plant_decay_day":0.02,"meat_decay_day":0.0,"reset_meat_each_year":true},"model_note":"Independent implementation of published FORGE human energy, effort and demographic equations with an explicitly simplified resource adapter. It is not the original ORCHIDEE-FORGE simulation, an agricultural carrying capacity, a c.200 AD census or a ranking of societal development. Terrestrial food is scaled by 0.5 and coastal and freshwater fish are a third food stock, both fitted on Binford's North American hunter-gatherers and tested on the rest of the world (reports/FORAGING